BT 72 720 Td 14 TL /F1 12 Tf (4 Depth, Oversmoothing, and Saturation) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (Stacking message-passing layers enlarges the receptive field but mixes node) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (representations until they become indistinguishable, a phenomenon known as) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (oversmoothing. The Weave-Depth protocol separates oversmoothing from) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (optimization difficulty by holding capacity fixed; the resulting curves show) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (that the depth-saturation cliff appears abruptly between twelve and sixteen) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (layers on seven of nine GraphWeave tasks.) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (Practical mitigations include residual connections, jumping knowledge style) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (readouts, and virtual nodes. None of these remove the cliff entirely; they) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (shift it by roughly two layers. Consequently, most successful applications) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (surveyed here use between two and six message-passing layers.) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (5 Open Problems) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (Three open problems recur across the surveyed literature. First, long-range) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (dependencies require depth that current architectures cannot sustain. Second,) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (evaluation protocols rarely separate expressivity from optimization. Third,) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (synthetic benchmarks like GraphWeave remain the only way to obtain controlled) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf (difficulty axes, yet they are underused.) Tj T* ET
endstream
